review:
The paper proposes TradeLens, a hybrid framework for parsing multi-party financial chats to extract RFQs, final prices, and hit/miss outcomes, combining small fine-tuned classifiers (RFQ detector, hit/miss, price NER) with a conversation-level engine (rule-based vs. RAG-style LLM) and a learned router that sends hard cases to a stronger LLM; contributions include the architecture, data- and cost-efficient engineering that shows fine-tuned open-source LLMs approach GPT-4o with limited data, and routing that cuts GPT-4o calls ~70% while keeping ~90% accuracy. LLM baselines are considered and compared: rule-based baseline, GPT-3.5/4/4o/4o-mini prompts, and fine-tuned open-source models.


Reasons to Accept:
1. Clear, practically important task with a modular design that marries small classifiers, RAG prompting, and routing for tangible ops gains; 
2. Strong empirical results with thorough ablations (prompt/query types, multiple GPT APIs, router variants) and meaningful cost/accuracy tradeoff analysis; 
3. Evidence that small fine-tuned open-source LLMs can nearly match closed-source models with little data;

Reasons to Reject
1. Evaluation is on private, narrow data (three chatrooms) with only accuracy metrics—limited external validity;  No clear release plan (code/models/prompts/dataset) to enable reproduction.
2. Heavy use of GPT-annotated “silver” labels risks bias/label leakage without robustness/out-of-domain checks; 
3. Limited analysis of error types, latency, and end-to-end compliance/privacy considerations;
